From: canoziia <canoziia@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mimo-v2: weight-streaming MXFP4 MoE decode kernels

VLLM_MIMO26_MXFP4_DECODE (default 0 = unchanged). For decode batches of at
most VLLM_MIMO26_MXFP4_DECODE_MAX_TOKENS (64) tokens, fused_marlin_moe runs
MiMo's two expert GEMMs with the DeepSeek V4.1 weight-streaming MXFP4 kernels
(deepseek-v4.1-flash 0004) instantiated for MiMo's experts (hidden 4096,
intermediate 2048) in their own library, _mimo26_moe_C
(models/mimo-v2.6-flash/native/mimo_moe, built into the image). Same
Marlin-packed weights and e8m0 scales, exact dequantisation, fixed-order
split-K partials; the activation is the caller's own and the slot sum stays
moe_sum. Only the fp32 accumulation order differs from Marlin: error against
an fp64 reference equals Marlin's (tests/test_mxfp4_decode_gpu.py), graph
replay equals eager, repeated calls are bitwise identical. Kernel time vs
Marlin at MiMo decode sizes: -3 % (M=1) to -11 % (M=16..32). The hook runs
after DeepSeek's, which declines MiMo's shapes.

VLLM_MIMO26_MXFP4_V2=1 uses the DeepSeek v2 kernels (deepseek-v4.1-flash
0008 plus the DQ=3 prototype, also instantiated for MiMo): split-K sum and
SiLU-mul fused into w13 (clamp limit +inf: bitwise equal to sum +
_C.silu_and_mul) and, for at most 16 tokens, the top-k sum fused into w2
(order 1: bitwise equal to moe_sum) - the v1 result bit for bit with two
fewer kernels per layer. VLLM_MIMO26_MXFP4_DQ=2 selects the cheaper dequant
(exact weights, different fp32 order, error equal to Marlin's). DQ=3 (integer
LUT/PRMT dequant) is bitwise equal to DQ0 but measured 1.8x slower here and
is not selectable. tests/test_mxfp4_decode_v2_gpu.py checks all of it.
---
